In Java both Comparable and Comparator are interfaces used to sort collections of objects
1. Comparable Interface

Purpose: Used to define the natural ordering of objects (default sorting logic)
Package: java.lang.Comparable
Method: compareTo(T obj)
Modifies the original class: The class itself must implement Comparable to define how its instances should be compared
Single Sorting logic: Only one compareTo() method can be defined for a class
Usage: Collections.sort(list)
Java 8+: can not use lambda

class Student implements Comparable<Student> {
    private String name;
    private int age;

    public Student(String name, int age) {
        this.name = name;
        this.age = age;
    }

    @Override
    public int compareTo(Student other) {
        return this.age - other.age; // Natural ordering by age
    }

    @Override
    public String toString() {
        return name + " (" + age + ")";
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Student> students = new ArrayList<>();
        students.add(new Student("Alice", 22));
        students.add(new Student("Bob", 20));
        students.add(new Student("Charlie", 25));

        Collections.sort(students); // Uses compareTo() for sorting

        System.out.println(students);
        // Output: [Bob (20), Alice (22), Charlie (25)]
    }
}

2. Comparator Interface

Purpose: Used to define custom ordering (multiple sorting logics) without modifying the original class
Package: java.util.Comparator
MethodL compare(T obj, T obj2)
External to the class: A separate class or lambda expression defines the comparison logic
Multiple sorting logics: Can define many Comparator implementations for different sorting needs
Usage: Collections.sort(list,comparator)
Java 8+: can use lambdas ((a,b)->a.age-b.age)

import java.util.Comparator;

class NameComparator implements Comparator<Student> {
    @Override
    public int compare(Student s1, Student s2) {
        return s1.getName().compareTo(s2.getName()); // Sort by name
    }
}

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Student> students = new ArrayList<>();
        students.add(new Student("Alice", 22));
        students.add(new Student("Bob", 20));
        students.add(new Student("Charlie", 25));

        // Sort using Comparator (by name)
        Collections.sort(students, new NameComparator());

        System.out.println(students);
        // Output: [Alice (22), Bob (20), Charlie (25)]
    }
}

// Ascending Comparator (natural order)
class AgeAscendingComparator implements Comparator<Student> {
    @Override
    public int compare(Student s1, Student s2) {
        return s1.getAge() - s2.getAge();
    }
}

// Descending Comparator
class AgeDescendingComparator implements Comparator<Student> {
    @Override
    public int compare(Student s1, Student s2) {
        return s2.getAge() - s1.getAge(); // Reverse order
    }
}

// Usage:
Collections.sort(students, new AgeAscendingComparator());  // Ascending
Collections.sort(students, new AgeDescendingComparator()); // Descending

Using Lambda (Java 8+)

public class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        List<Student> students = new ArrayList<>();
        students.add(new Student("Alice", 22));
        students.add(new Student("Bob", 20));
        students.add(new Student("Charlie", 25));

        // Sort by age using lambda
        Collections.sort(students, (s1, s2) -> s1.getAge() - s2.getAge());

        // Sort by name using lambda
        Collections.sort(students, (s1, s2) -> s1.getName().compareTo(s2.getName()));

        // Using Comparator.comparing()
        students.sort(Comparator.comparing(Student::getName)); // Sort by name
        students.sort(Comparator.comparingInt(Student::getAge)); // Sort by age
    }
}


3. Sorting array in Java

Using Arrays.sort() provided by java.util.Arrays
a) Sorting an array of integers in ascending order
int [] numbers={5,6,2,1};
Arrays.sort(numbers);

String[] names = {"John", "Alice", "Bob", "David"};

// Sorting the array in alphabetical order
Arrays.sort(names);

b) Sorting in Descending order
Integer[] numbers = {5, 2, 8, 3, 1};

// Sorting the array in descending order
Arrays.sort(numbers, Collections.reverseOrder());

Note: If you are dealing with primitive types like int, they can not directly sorted in descending order using
Collections.reverseOrder(). You need to use a wrapper class like Integer [] instead of int []
Arrays.sort() can not directly accept a comparator for primitive types. We can use reverse sort with a custom method

int[] numbers = {5, 2, 8, 3, 1};

// Sorting the array in ascending order first
Arrays.sort(numbers);

// Reversing the array to make it descending
reverse(numbers);

public static void reverse(int[] array) {
        int left = 0;
        int right = array.length - 1;

        // Swap the elements
        while (left < right) {
            int temp = array[left];
            array[left] = array[right];
            array[right] = temp;
            left++;
            right--;
        }
    }

Descending sorting with wrapper class

// Initial primitive int array
        int[] numbers = {5, 2, 8, 3, 1};

// Convert the primitive int[] to Integer[] (wrapper class)
Integer[] boxedNumbers = Arrays.stream(numbers)  // Convert int[] to Stream<Integer>
                                .boxed()           // Box each element to Integer
                                .toArray(Integer[]::new); // Collect into Integer[]

// Sorting the array in descending order
Arrays.sort(boxedNumbers, Collections.reverseOrder());

For large dataset we need to use QuickSort or MergeSort